// v3.compiler.tokenize — tokenizer authority for SG-1.
//
// The Rust projection (`tokenize_generated.rs`) is produced by
// `cargo run -p v3-compiler --bin regen_tokenize` — do not hand-edit it.
//
// **Authority boundary.** Shared token types live in
// `src/v3/std/tokenize.dag`. This file declares tokenizer-local punctuation
// rows not modeled in `dsl/extdeps/languages/dag/syntax.dag`, string escape
// behavior, line-comment prefix, string delimiter, and fixed tokenizer
// diagnostic bodies. `regen_tokenize` derives the dedicated keyword subset and
// the shared-operator punctuation subset from the shared syntax authority at
// `dsl/extdeps/languages/dag/syntax.dag`, then merges those with the
// tokenizer-local rows below. That keeps authored spellings for keywords /
// shared operators out of this file.
//
// **Tracked scaffold (SG-1a).** The shared authority handoff is not yet a pure
// structural read: `regen_tokenize` currently parses the raw source text for
// `dag_keyword_set` / `dag_operators` because those bodies still lower as
// `ValueBody::Unparsed`. Named dissolution trigger: once those two shared
// syntax bodies lower structurally under `compile_to_dag`, delete the raw-text
// extractor and derive directly from the lowered Dag in the same PR.
//
// **Tracked scaffold (character-level under-consumption — consumption gap,
// not substrate gap).** The scan phases below slice the ASCII/Unicode
// codepoint space in two parallel forms, neither of which consumes the
// character-level authorities that already exist in `dsl/std/`:
//
//   1. **Reserved individual codepoints as opaque strings.** `StringEscapeSpec`
//      fields (`suffix: String`, `output_codepoint: Int`), `LocalPunctSpec.
//      pattern: String`, `string_literal_delimiter: String`,
//      `line_comment_prefix: String` — all encode one or more specific
//      codepoints as byte strings. Parallel authority in
//      `dsl/extdeps/languages/dag/syntax.dag` does the same for operators
//      (`OperatorSpec.symbol: String`) and keywords
//      (`dag_keyword_set: Map<String, Bool>` keys).
//
//   2. **Reserved codepoint classes as hidden Rust predicates.**
//      `regen_tokenize.rs` `push_str`s `is_ascii_whitespace` /
//      `is_ascii_digit` / `is_ascii_alphabetic` / `is_ascii_alphanumeric`
//      plus a bare `byte == b'_'` into the generated tokenizer. Not mentioned
//      in `.dag` at all.
//
// **Existing authorities to consume** (already imported cross-tree today —
// e.g. `src/v3/compiler/regen.dag:3` imports `std.types`):
//   - `std.types::Char = Int` (Unicode scalar, U+0000–U+10FFFF).
//   - `std.string_type::String = FreeMonoid<Char>` (String already decomposes).
//   - `std.unicode` (`DisplayWidth`, `UnicodeBlock`, block/width classification).
//   - `std.encoding` (`Encoding = ASCII | UTF8 | Latin1 | Text | Binary |
//     Unknown` lattice with `ASCII <: UTF8 <: Text` — literally the
//     ASCII→Unicode→… causal chain).
//   - `std.bit::Byte`.
//
// **CharClass — tokenizer half (bounded interim, not full lane closure).**
// `std.unicode` declares `CharClass` + `char_in_class` (canonical `.dag`
// authority). `ascii_scan_order` makes tokenizer scanner precedence a
// structural `List<CharClass>` consumed directly by `regen_tokenize`; ASCII
// predicate bodies remain a bounded generator bridge until `char_in_class`
// semantics are structurally consumed.
//
// **Framing.** Phase 1 was a consumption gap: the character-level modeling was
// already present, but tokenize.dag and syntax.dag encoded the same activity
// (slicing the ASCII/Unicode space) in non-canonical forms (opaque strings,
// hidden Rust predicates) instead of consuming the canonical `Char` /
// `std.unicode` authorities. Phase 2's substrate/load-set prerequisites are:
// scanner rows such as `data ascii_scan_order: List<CharClass> = [...]` need
// top-level structural `data` bodies for lists/sum constructors, and
// `std.unicode` must be in the runtime bootstrap/load set before `CharClass`
// resolves from this compiler authority. Named dissolution trigger (tracked in
// ROADMAP as a follow-up lane): retype the opaque-string fields in this file and
// `syntax.dag` to consume `Char` / `List<Char>`, author the scanner class list,
// then rewire `regen_tokenize` to read it structurally rather than hardcoding
// host-stdlib method names. Goal is
// faithful consumption of an existing borrow, not re-implementing Unicode in
// `.dag`.
//
// **Scan phases (spec).** After skipping ASCII whitespace, the scanner applies
// in order: signed unary `-` merged into a following decimal `IntLit` when the
// previous token is **not** listed in `minus_infix_only_after_token_kinds`
// (otherwise `-` is infix `Minus`), line comment (`line_comment_prefix` to newline),
// punctuation (longest match across the shared-operator subset plus local punctuation
// rows), ASCII digit run → decimal `IntLit`, identifier run → keyword table or
// `Ident`, string (`string_literal_delimiter`) with `StringEscapeSpec` rows
// after `\`, else `unexpected byte` diagnostic.

module v3.compiler.tokenize

import v3.std.tokenize {
  KeywordTokenKind,
  LocalPunctSpec,
  PunctTokenKind,
  StringEscapeSpec,
  Token,
  TokenKind
}

import v3.std.unicode {
  CharClass
}

// Scanner order is now structurally owned by tokenizer authority. Predicate
// bodies still live in `regen_tokenize` until `char_in_class` is structurally
// executable for this path.
data ascii_scan_order: List<CharClass> = [Whitespace, Digit, IdentStart, IdentContinue]

// `TokenKind` variant labels (`src/v3/std/tokenize.dag`): when the **previous** token
// has one of these shapes, a `-` must **not** merge into the following ASCII digit run
// (tokenizes as infix `Minus`). `regen_tokenize` emits `minus_prefixed_decimal_allowed`
// solely from this list — keep tokenizer facts single-authority in `tokenize.dag`.
data minus_infix_only_after_token_kinds: List<String> = [
  "Ident",
  "IntLit",
  "StringLit",
  "KwTrue",
  "KwFalse",
  "RParen",
  "RBracket",
  "RBrace"
]

// Fixed diagnostic message bodies for tokenizer failures (C-8: errors only).
data diagnostic_unterminated_string_escape: String = "unterminated string escape"
data diagnostic_unterminated_string_literal: String = "unterminated string literal"

// `format!("invalid integer literal `{}`", literal)` — prefix/suffix split so
// the delimiter is explicit in data without nested braces in one string.
data diagnostic_invalid_integer_literal_prefix: String = "invalid integer literal `"
data diagnostic_invalid_integer_literal_suffix: String = "`"

// Line comment begins with this two-byte prefix; scanner runs to `\n` or EOF.
data line_comment_prefix: String = "//"

// Opening delimiter for string literals (one ASCII character).
data string_literal_delimiter: String = "\""

data string_escape_dquote: StringEscapeSpec = { suffix: "\"", output_codepoint: 34 }
data string_escape_backslash: StringEscapeSpec = { suffix: "\\", output_codepoint: 92 }
data string_escape_n: StringEscapeSpec = { suffix: "n", output_codepoint: 10 }
data string_escape_r: StringEscapeSpec = { suffix: "r", output_codepoint: 13 }
data string_escape_t: StringEscapeSpec = { suffix: "t", output_codepoint: 9 }

data local_punct_eq: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "=", kind: Eq }
data local_punct_colon: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: ":", kind: Colon }
data local_punct_arrow: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "->", kind: Arrow }
data local_punct_fat_arrow: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "=>", kind: FatArrow }
data local_punct_lparen: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "(", kind: LParen }
data local_punct_rparen: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: ")", kind: RParen }
data local_punct_lbrace: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "{", kind: LBrace }
data local_punct_rbrace: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "}", kind: RBrace }
data local_punct_lbracket: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "[", kind: LBracket }
data local_punct_rbracket: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "]", kind: RBracket }
data local_punct_comma: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: ",", kind: Comma }
data local_punct_semicolon: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: ";", kind: Semicolon }
data local_punct_pipe: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "|", kind: Pipe }
data local_punct_question: LocalPunctSpec = { pattern: "?", kind: Question }
